/* data/detail-c/phy-11-04.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/phy-11-04.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-11-04"] = {"phy-11-04-m01":{"idea":"Galileo saw that a ball on a smooth slope keeps going. Only opposing forces make it stop.","steps":["Roll a ball on a rough floor and it stops soon. On a smoother floor it goes farther.","Galileo reasoned that with no friction, it would never stop.","So the stopping is caused by friction and other opposing forces."],"others":"Gravity acts downwards on a level surface and does not slow a horizontal motion. Rest is not the natural state of a moving body, as Galileo showed. A force is needed to change motion, not to keep it going.","trap":"The old idea (force is needed to keep motion) is what Galileo corrected.","answer":"friction and other opposing forces act on it"},"phy-11-04-m02":{"idea":"The first law defines inertia: a body keeps its state of motion unless a force changes it.","steps":["With no net force, a body at rest stays at rest.","With no net force, a moving body keeps its speed and its direction.","Only an unbalanced force can change this state."],"others":"Acceleration proportional to speed is not any law of Newton. Motion does not need a force to continue, so a body does not stop when the force is removed. A body does not come to rest by itself, unless friction acts.","trap":"No force does not mean no motion. It means no change of motion.","answer":"stays at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line, unless an unbalanced force acts on it"},"phy-11-04-m03":{"idea":"Newton's second law links the force to how fast the momentum changes.","steps":["Momentum is p = mv.","The force is the rate of change of momentum: F = dp/dt.","For a constant mass, this gives F = ma."],"others":"A change of velocity alone is not a force. m/a has the wrong form, since force grows with a. Momentum times velocity has the units of energy-like quantities, not force.","trap":"F = ma is a special case of F = dp/dt for a constant mass.","answer":"the rate of change of its momentum, F = dp/dt"},"phy-11-04-m04":{"idea":"Find the acceleration from F = ma, then the velocity from v = u + at.","steps":["a = F/m = 20/5 = 4 m/s².","The body starts from rest, so u = 0.","v = 0 + 4 × 3 = 12 m/s."],"others":"1 m/s is wrong since it ignores the time. 4 m/s is only the acceleration. 15 m/s and 60 m/s come from using the force or the mass in the wrong place.","trap":"The velocity needs the time: v = at, not just a.","answer":"12 m/s"},"phy-11-04-m05":{"idea":"Action and reaction are equal and opposite. They act on two different bodies.","steps":["If body 1 pushes body 2 with a force F, body 2 pushes body 1 with −F.","The two forces are equal in size and opposite in direction.","They act on different bodies, so they never cancel each other."],"others":"The forces are never unequal. They are opposite, not in the same direction. If both acted on the same body, they would cancel, but they act on different bodies.","trap":"Cancelling needs both forces on the same body. Action and reaction are on two bodies.","answer":"are equal and opposite, and act on different bodies"},"phy-11-04-m06":{"idea":"The reaction of the wall acts on the person. It is the same size as the push.","steps":["The person pushes the wall with a force F.","By the third law, the wall pushes the person back with the force F.","This force acts on the person, not on the wall."],"others":"The reaction does not act on the wall, so it does not cancel the push on the wall. Not moving does not mean a zero reaction. The wall's reaction cannot be bigger than the push, as the two are always equal.","trap":"A heavy wall does not push harder. The third law gives equal forces.","answer":"is equal in size to the push of the person, and acts on the person"},"phy-11-04-m07":{"idea":"Newton's laws work in some frames and not in others. Those where they work are inertial.","steps":["An inertial frame has no acceleration.","It is at rest, or moves in a straight line at a constant velocity.","In it, a body with no force on it keeps its velocity."],"others":"A frame fixed to the Earth is only nearly inertial, but 'only' is the problem: any frame moving steadily works. An accelerating frame is not inertial. A steadily rotating frame is also not inertial, as it is accelerating.","trap":"Any frame moving at constant velocity relative to an inertial frame is also inertial.","answer":"is at rest or moves with a constant velocity, and Newton's laws hold in it"},"phy-11-04-m08":{"idea":"A pseudo force is added by hand, so that Newton's second law can be used in an accelerating frame.","steps":["In a frame with acceleration a, we add a force −ma on each body.","This force comes from the motion of the frame and not from another body.","So it has no reaction partner."],"others":"Gravity is a real force with a reaction partner. In an inertial frame, there is no pseudo force. A pseudo force is not tied to contact between two bodies.","trap":"Pseudo forces appear only in non-inertial frames.","answer":"in a non-inertial frame, and it has no reaction partner"},"phy-11-04-m09":{"idea":"In an upward-accelerating lift, the floor must push up harder than the weight.","steps":["Forces on the person: the normal force N upward and the weight mg downward.","Newton's second law, with the upward direction positive: N − mg = ma.","So N = mg + ma. This is the scale reading."],"others":"The reading mg − ma is for a lift with a downward acceleration. ma leaves out the weight. mg is for a lift at rest or at a constant speed.","trap":"Going up and speeding up: you feel heavier.","answer":"m(g + a)"},"phy-11-04-m10":{"idea":"In free fall, the person and the lift both have an acceleration g. The floor need not push at all.","steps":["N − mg = m(−g) for the downward acceleration g.","N = mg − mg = 0.","The scale reads zero: the person feels weightless."],"others":"m/g does not have the units of force. 2mg would need a lift accelerating up with g. mg is for a lift at rest, but the lift is falling.","trap":"Free fall means weightless, but gravity has not gone away.","answer":"zero, as N = m(g − g)"},"phy-11-04-m11":{"idea":"The four forces differ greatly in strength. Gravity is by far the weakest.","steps":["In order of strength: strong nuclear, electromagnetic, weak nuclear, gravitational.","Gravity looks strong to us only because the Earth is so large.","Between two protons it is about 10³⁶ times weaker than the electric force."],"others":"The weak nuclear force is weak, but it is much stronger than gravity. The electromagnetic force is far stronger than gravity. The strong nuclear force is the strongest.","trap":"'Weak force' is a name. Gravity is the really weakest.","answer":"the gravitational force"},"phy-11-04-m12":{"idea":"All the contact forces we see in daily life come from the electric forces between atoms.","steps":["Friction, tension and the normal reaction are all push and pull between surfaces.","Surfaces are made of atoms, and atoms contain charges.","So these forces are of electromagnetic origin."],"others":"Strong nuclear forces act only inside the nucleus. Weak nuclear forces are involved in radioactive decay, not in contact forces. Gravity is too weak to cause these forces between small objects.","trap":"Contact force means electric force between atoms.","answer":"electromagnetic origin, between the charges in the atoms"},"phy-11-04-m13":{"idea":"The work-energy theorem links the net work to the change in speed.","steps":["Net work on a body = F × s = m a s.","Using v² = u² + 2as, this becomes ½mv² − ½mu².","So the net work equals the change in kinetic energy."],"others":"Potential energy is linked only to conservative forces, not to net work in general. Final kinetic energy alone leaves out the starting value. Momentum change is the impulse, not the work.","trap":"Work changes kinetic energy. Impulse changes momentum.","answer":"the change in its kinetic energy"},"phy-11-04-m14":{"idea":"Work is F d cos θ. A force at right angles to the motion does no work.","steps":["W = F d cos θ, and θ = 90°.","cos 90° = 0.","So W = 0."],"others":"F × distance is correct only when the force is along the motion. The work is not negative, since that needs the force to oppose the motion. It is the least, not the maximum.","trap":"Examples: the centripetal force, and the normal reaction on a level floor.","answer":"zero, as cos 90° = 0"},"phy-11-04-m15":{"idea":"A system is free of change in momentum if no outside force acts on it.","steps":["F_ext = dP/dt for the system.","If F_ext = 0, then dP/dt = 0.","So the total momentum stays constant."],"others":"Kinetic energy is not always constant, for example in sticky collisions. A system can have any number of bodies. Internal forces are always in pairs that cancel, so they do not decide this.","trap":"Internal forces cannot change the total momentum.","answer":"the net external force on the system is zero"},"phy-11-04-m16":{"idea":"Momentum is conserved in all collisions without external force. Kinetic energy is not.","steps":["The collision forces are internal to the two bodies, and they cancel in pairs.","So the total momentum is the same before and after.","Kinetic energy is conserved only in elastic collisions."],"others":"The kinetic energy of each body changes in a collision. The velocity of each body changes. The total kinetic energy is not conserved when some of it becomes heat or sound.","trap":"Momentum always. Kinetic energy only if the collision is elastic.","answer":"the total linear momentum, if no external force acts"},"phy-11-04-m17":{"idea":"In a perfectly inelastic collision, the bodies move together after the collision.","steps":["They stick together and move with one common velocity.","This gives the greatest possible loss of kinetic energy.","Momentum is still conserved."],"others":"Bouncing apart does not mean the greatest loss. Sticking together with the kinetic energy conserved is impossible, as the energy is lost in sticking. The bounce and the conservation of energy describe an elastic collision.","trap":"Perfectly inelastic: e = 0, common velocity.","answer":"stick together, and the loss of kinetic energy is the greatest"},"phy-11-04-m18":{"idea":"Equal masses in a head-on elastic collision swap their velocities.","steps":["Momentum: mu = mv₁ + mv₂. Energy: ½mu² = ½mv₁² + ½mv₂².","The solution is v₁ = 0 and v₂ = u.","The first body stops and the second moves off with u."],"others":"A rebound with speed u is for a light body that hits a very heavy one. Both bodies moving with u would double the momentum. Both with u/2 would be a perfectly inelastic collision, with a loss of energy.","trap":"Billiard balls: the cue ball stops and the other ball goes off.","answer":"the first body stops, and the second moves with the speed u"},"phy-11-04-m19":{"idea":"The coefficient of restitution is the ratio of the speed of separation to the speed of approach.","steps":["e = speed of separation / speed of approach.","In an elastic collision, e = 1.","In a perfectly inelastic collision, e = 0. All other collisions lie between 0 and 1."],"others":"e cannot be larger than 1, since that would create energy. e = 0 is for the perfectly inelastic case, not the elastic one. A negative e would mean the bodies pass through one another.","trap":"e = 1 elastic, e = 0 sticking, in between for the others.","answer":"0 to 1, with e = 1 for an elastic collision"},"phy-11-04-m20":{"idea":"Impulse is the force multiplied by the time that it acts. It equals the change of momentum.","steps":["Impulse J = F Δt.","From F = Δp/Δt, J = Δp.","So impulse equals the change in momentum."],"others":"A change in kinetic energy is the work done, not the impulse. Force divided by time does not give a momentum quantity. The work done is force times distance, not force times time.","trap":"Impulse is in N s, the same as kg m/s.","answer":"the change in the momentum of the body"},"phy-11-04-m21":{"idea":"The same change of momentum with more time gives a smaller average force.","steps":["The ball must lose its momentum: Δp is fixed.","F = Δp / Δt, so a longer Δt gives a smaller F.","Pulling the hands back makes the contact time longer."],"others":"The momentum of the ball does not increase. The mass of the ball does not change. A shorter contact time would make the force larger, not smaller.","trap":"Same idea: airbags, and landing with bent knees.","answer":"increases the time of contact, so the average force on the hands is smaller"},"phy-11-04-m22":{"idea":"Torque is the turning effect of a force. It is the force times the perpendicular distance.","steps":["The distance d is measured from the axis, at right angles to the line of the force.","Torque τ = F × d.","Its unit is N m."],"others":"F + d adds two unlike quantities, which is meaningless. F/d would fall as the arm gets longer. F d² has an extra power of d.","trap":"A longer spanner gives a larger torque for the same force.","answer":"τ = F d"},"phy-11-04-m23":{"idea":"If the line of the force passes through the axis, the perpendicular distance is zero.","steps":["τ = F × d, where d is the perpendicular distance from the axis.","The line goes through the axis, so d = 0.","So τ = 0."],"others":"The torque is not infinite, since 0 times F is 0. It is not at the maximum, as the force is not turning the body. It does not equal the force, as it has other units.","trap":"Pushing a door along its hinge line does not turn it.","answer":"zero, as the lever arm is zero"},"phy-11-04-m24":{"idea":"A couple is two forces that turn a body but do not push it as a whole.","steps":["The two forces are equal in size and opposite in direction.","Their lines of action are different, with a gap between them.","The net force is zero, but the net torque is not zero."],"others":"Equal forces in the same direction would push the body forward. Opposite forces along the same line cancel completely. Unequal opposite forces would also give a net force.","trap":"Turning a steering wheel with two hands is a couple.","answer":"equal, opposite, and with different lines of action"},"phy-11-04-m25":{"idea":"A rigid body is in mechanical equilibrium if it has no push and no twist.","steps":["Translation equilibrium: the net force is zero.","Rotation equilibrium: the net torque is zero.","Both conditions are needed."],"others":"Zero torque alone allows the body to speed up in a line. Zero force alone allows it to spin faster, as with a couple. Being at rest at one instant does not give equilibrium if the forces are unbalanced.","trap":"Two conditions: ΣF = 0 and Στ = 0.","answer":"the net force and the net torque on it are both zero"},"phy-11-04-m26":{"idea":"A stable body goes back when it is displaced. This happens when it sits at the bottom of a potential energy well.","steps":["At a minimum of the potential energy, any small move raises the energy.","The body then goes back to lower the energy.","So stable equilibrium has the potential energy at a minimum."],"others":"Zero potential energy does not by itself give stability. A constant energy means neutral equilibrium. A maximum gives unstable equilibrium, where a small push makes the body fall away.","trap":"Ball in a bowl: stable. Ball on top of a dome: unstable.","answer":"a minimum, so a small displacement makes it return"},"phy-11-04-m27":{"idea":"The centre of mass lies closer to the heavier body.","steps":["With masses m₁ and m₂, the centre of mass is where m₁x₁ + m₂x₂ is balanced.","The heavier mass pulls the point toward itself.","So the point is closer to the 3 kg particle, on the line between the two."],"others":"It is not closer to the lighter 1 kg particle. It does not lie outside the line joining two particles. It would be at the middle only for equal masses.","trap":"Seesaw: the heavier child sits closer to the pivot.","answer":"closer to the 3 kg particle, on the line joining them"},"phy-11-04-m28":{"idea":"The centre of mass of a semicircular disc is found by integration. The result is a standard formula.","steps":["By symmetry, it lies on the axis through the middle of the straight edge.","Its distance from the centre of the edge is 4R/(3π).","This is about 0.42 R."],"others":"2R/π is the result for a semicircular wire (arc), not for a disc. 3R/8 is the result for a solid hemisphere. R/2 is a guess with no basis.","trap":"Wire arc: 2R/π. Disc: 4R/3π. Solid hemisphere: 3R/8.","answer":"4R/(3π), along the axis of symmetry"},"phy-11-04-m29":{"idea":"The centre of gravity is the point where the weight seems to act. It matches the centre of mass if g is the same everywhere.","steps":["Centre of mass uses the masses alone.","Centre of gravity uses the weights, which depend on g at each point.","If g is uniform over the body, the two points are the same."],"others":"A body at rest on the ground does not decide this. A regular shape does not matter either, if g changes over the body. A point mass is a trivial case, but it is not the general condition.","trap":"For very tall bodies, g changes, and the two points can differ a little.","answer":"the gravitational field is uniform over the body"},"phy-11-04-n01":{"idea":"Use Newton's second law, F = ma.","steps":["m = 5 kg, a = 3 m/s².","F = 5 × 3.","F = 15.0 N."],"trap":"The 4 s is not needed: the force depends only on m and a.","answer":"15.0 N"},"phy-11-04-n02":{"idea":"In an Atwood machine, the difference of the weights moves the whole mass.","steps":["Net force = (3 − 2) × 10 = 10 N.","Total mass moving = 2 + 3 = 5 kg.","a = 10/5 = 2.0 m/s²."],"trap":"If the masses were equal, a would be zero.","answer":"2.0 m/s²"},"phy-11-04-n03":{"idea":"Use the lighter mass. The tension has to lift it with the acceleration.","steps":["For the 2 kg mass, going up: T − mg = ma.","T = 2 × 10 + 2 × 2 = 24 N.","Check with the 3 kg mass: 3 × 10 − T = 3 × 2, so T = 24 N."],"trap":"T lies between 20 N (weight of 2 kg) and 30 N (weight of 3 kg). 24 N fits.","answer":"24.0 N"},"phy-11-04-n04":{"idea":"In an upward-accelerating lift, the scale reads m times the sum of g and a.","steps":["N − mg = ma, so N = mg + ma.","N = 60 × (10 + 2).","N = 720 N."],"trap":"At rest, the reading would be 600 N. It is higher when the lift goes up with an acceleration.","answer":"720 N"},"phy-11-04-n05":{"idea":"Use momentum conservation to find the common speed. Then compare the kinetic energies.","steps":["2 × 6 = (2 + 4) v, so v = 2 m/s.","KE before = ½ × 2 × 6² = 36 J. KE after = ½ × 6 × 2² = 12 J.","Loss = 36 − 12 = 24.0 J."],"trap":"The loss is two thirds of the starting energy, since m₂/(m₁ + m₂) = 4/6.","answer":"24.0 J"},"phy-11-04-n06":{"idea":"For an elastic collision with the target at rest, v₂ = 2m₁u/(m₁ + m₂).","steps":["m₁ = 2 kg, m₂ = 4 kg, u = 6 m/s.","v₂ = 2 × 2 × 6 / (2 + 4) = 24/6.","v₂ = 4.0 m/s."],"trap":"Check: v₁ = (2 − 4)/6 × 6 = −2 m/s. Momentum: 12 = 2(−2) + 4(4) = 12.","answer":"4.0 m/s"},"phy-11-04-n07":{"idea":"Impulse is the change in momentum. The direction reverses, so the speeds add.","steps":["Take the final direction as positive: the initial velocity is −12 m/s, the final is +20 m/s.","Δv = 20 − (−12) = 32 m/s.","Impulse = m Δv = 0.15 × 32 = 4.8 N s."],"trap":"Do not subtract 12 from 20. The ball turns round, so the change is 32.","answer":"4.8 N s"},"phy-11-04-n08":{"idea":"The net work equals the change in kinetic energy.","steps":["W = ½m(v² − u²).","= ½ × 1000 × (20² − 10²).","= 500 × 300 = 150000 J."],"trap":"Do not use ½m(v − u)². The squares come first, then subtract.","answer":"150000 J"},"phy-11-04-n09":{"idea":"Torque is F d sin θ, where θ is the angle between the force and the spanner.","steps":["F = 20 N, r = 0.50 m, θ = 30°.","τ = r F sin θ = 0.50 × 20 × 0.5.","τ = 5.00 N m."],"trap":"The torque is half the largest value (10 N m at 90°), since sin 30° = 0.5.","answer":"5.00 N m"},"phy-11-04-n10":{"idea":"The moment of a couple is one force times the perpendicular distance between the forces.","steps":["F = 15 N, d = 0.40 m.","Moment = F × d.","= 15 × 0.40 = 6.0 N m."],"trap":"Do not use 2F. The two forces together give F × d, not 2F × d.","answer":"6.0 N m"},"phy-11-04-n11":{"idea":"The centre of mass is the mass-weighted average of the positions.","steps":["x_cm = (m₁x₁ + m₂x₂ + m₃x₃) / (m₁ + m₂ + m₃).","= (1 × 0 + 2 × 1 + 3 × 3) / (1 + 2 + 3).","= 11/6 = 1.833 m."],"trap":"The answer lies between 0 and 3 m, and closer to the heavy end.","answer":"1.833 m"},"phy-11-04-n12":{"idea":"The centre of mass of a semicircular disc is at 4R/(3π) from the centre of its straight edge.","steps":["R = 0.30 m.","x = 4R/(3π) = 4 × 0.30 / (3 × 3.1416).","x = 1.2 / 9.4248 = 0.127 m."],"trap":"It is about 0.42 R, which is 0.126 m. It is close.","answer":"0.127 m"},"phy-11-04-n13":{"idea":"For equilibrium, take torques about one support. That removes one unknown force.","steps":["Take torques about the right support. The plank's weight 300 N acts at the middle, 3 m away. The person's weight 600 N is 4 m away.","R_L × 6 = 300 × 3 + 600 × 4 = 900 + 2400 = 3300.","R_L = 550 N."],"trap":"Check: the right reaction is 900 − 550 = 350 N. Take torques about the left support: 350 × 6 = 2100 = 300 × 3 + 600 × 2.","answer":"550 N"},"phy-11-04-n14":{"idea":"Momentum is conserved. The gun and the bullet start at rest, so the total is zero.","steps":["0 = m_bullet × v_bullet + M_gun × v_gun.","v_gun = 0.05 × 300 / 5.","v_gun = 3.0 m/s, in the opposite direction."],"trap":"The gun moves back slowly because it is 100 times heavier than the bullet.","answer":"3.0 m/s"}};
